9, I could form a chow T6,T7,T8 or T7,T8,T9, but then I'd still need a pair. With three T7, I could use two T7 as a pair and one T7 as part of the chow? But the chow would use one T7, leaving two T7 as a pair \u2014 that also works: chow (T6,T7,T8) + pair T7. That's also winning. So discarding B4 seems flexible: I can win by drawing T8 (for pair) or T6/T9 (for chow that yields a pair from T7).  \n\nCheck availability: T6 unshown: 3 left (none in my hand, not in melds much? Next player has B6 in a chow B6,B7,B8, but that's B6, not T6. So T6 available). T8 unshown: 4 total, I hold 1, so 3 left. T9 unshown: 2 left. So all are available.  \n\nDiscarding T8 leaves me only the out of drawing B4 for pair. Discarding T7 leaves me needing more complicated draws. Discarding B4 gives multiple outs: T6, T8, T9 (and possibly others? also drawing a T7 is impossible as none left). So discarding B4 maximizes winning chances.  \n\nAlso consider hand value: If I win with pung of T7, I could have \"All Chows\" (since my other melds are chows) but pung T7 breaks that, so no All Chows. But I might have \"Pung of Terminals or Honors\"? T7 is not terminal/honor, so no. \"All Simples\"? T7 is simple, B4 is simple, all tiles are simples, so that's 2 points. \"Concealed Pung\" for T7? Possibly if I keep it concealed. But I have exposed chows, so not fully concealed. Still, some points likely.  \n\nGiven the need to maximize winning probability, discarding B4 is best.  \n\nReason: Discarding B4 keeps three T7 and one T8, providing multiple drawing outs to complete the hand: drawing T8 gives a pair T8 and a pung T7; drawing T6 or T9 allows forming a chow (T6,T7,T8 or T7,T8,T9) and using two T7 as a pair. Both scenarios complete a standard hand (four melds + pair). Discarding T8 or T7 reduces outs and makes the hand less flexible.  \n\nAnswer: Play B4"}
